<Base title="The boot" subtitle={kitPage('boots').summary} crumbs={crumbs}>
  <div class="crumbs" role="navigation" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><p class="lede"><a href={url('kit/')}>Kit</a></p></div>
  <h1>The boot</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A figure boot holds the ankle upright over a narrow blade and still lets the knee and
    shin bend forward. Most of what matters about one is how well it does both for you.
  </p>

  <h2>Fit</h2>
  <p>
    The heel should sit firmly at the back of the boot and stay there when you bend your
    knee and rise again. The toes should be able to move, but the foot should not slide
    forward. Skating boots are often sized differently from street shoes, so a fitting at a
    skate shop is worth more than the number on your trainers. A boot that is too big is
    the usual mistake, made so that a growing skater can grow into it, and it costs balance
    long before it fits.
  </p>

  <h2>Stiffness</h2>
  <p>
    Boots come in a range of stiffness, matched to the skater's weight and to what they
    skate. A boot that is too soft for the skater folds at the ankle and lets the edges go.
    One that is too stiff is the opposite problem and just as common: the shin cannot lean
    forward far enough, so the knee cannot bend, and every element that lives on a bent
    knee suffers. The figures in this guide assume a boot that lets the shin lean about 28
    degrees forward, and a skater in a boot that allows much less cannot make the shapes
    they show.
  </p>
  <p>
    A stiffer boot is not a better boot. It is the right boot once the skater's jumps and
    weight need it, and a coach or a fitter can say when that is.
  </p>

  <h2>Breaking in and lacing</h2>
  <p>
    New boots are stiff and take some sessions to soften where the ankle bends. Some can be
    heat-moulded to the foot when they are fitted. Lace firmly across the foot and the
    ankle, so the heel stays down, and a little looser over the top hooks, so the shin can
    still lean forward. Laces pulled tight all the way to the top make even a soft boot too
    stiff.
  </p>

  <h2>The blade on the boot</h2>
  <p>
    Blades are bought separately on most boots beyond the first, and mounted by a
    technician. Where the blade sits under the boot, a few millimetres either way, changes
    how the skater balances over it, which is why the mounting is worth having done by
    someone who does it often. The <a href={url('kit/blade/')}>blade page</a> has the shape
    of the blade itself.
  </p>
</Base>
